  "text": "In a retrospective study of 428 critically-ill patients with severe sepsis or septic shock, pulmonary and abdominal infections predominated, in keeping with our findings [25]. The initial infection escaped identification in nearly 20% of our patients, as reported previously [28]. An increase over time in GNB infections among neutropenic patients with cancer and sepsis has been reported [28]. GNB infections predominated in our cohort but did not increase significantly between the early and recent periods. Multidrug-resistant pathogens contributed fewer than 5% of the organisms recovered in our patients compared to nearly 25% in a 2010–2017 study from Spain confined to patients with hematological malignancies [29], reflecting differences in epidemiology between the two countries."
}

