{
  "paraId": "3DAAC3F5",
  "style": "",
  "text": "In a retrospective study of 428 critically-ill patients with severe sepsis or septic shock, pulmonary and abdominal infections predominated, in keeping with our findings [25]. The initial infection escaped identification in nearly 20% of our patients, as reported previously [28]. An increase over time in GNB infections among neutropenic patients with cancer and sepsis has been reported [28]. GNB infections predominated in our cohort but did not increase significantly between the early and recent periods. Multidrug-resistant pathogens contributed fewer than 5% of the organisms recovered in our patients compared to nearly 25% in a 2010–2017 study from Spain confined to patients with hematological malignancies [29], reflecting differences in epidemiology between the two countries. In the 428 ICU patients with cancer, neutropenia, and severe sepsis or septic shock studied by Legrand et al. between 1998 and 2008, hospital mortality was 49.8% overall and fell from 58.7% (108/184) in 1998–2003 to 43% (105/244) in 2004–2008 (p = 0.006), a temporal pattern consistent with the decline from 49.4% to 40.0% seen in our cohort [25]."
}

